"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][31]={"university_physics_en:M31-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M31-L1 – Time integration, stiffness and stability<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Master: Numerical and Computational Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12: differential equations; M13:\nvariational principles; M20: numerical foundations · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the explicit Euler\nstep-size stability condition from a test equation and select suitable\ntime integrators.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>An equation of motion becomes computationally useful when its time\nevolution is evaluated with controlled error. Explicit integrators are\nsimple but sensitive to the step size for rapidly decaying modes.\nStiffness means that stability can require time steps far smaller than\nthose needed merely for the intended temporal resolution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For the test equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\prime=-\\lambda y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand initial value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe exact solution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=y_0e^{-\\lambda t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nExplicit Euler yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_{n+1}=y_n-h\\lambda y_n=(1-h\\lambda)y_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBecause every step uses the same amplification factor, its magnitude\nmust remain below one. For this scalar problem with positive real\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;h\\lambda&lt;2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a stability condition, not a guarantee of high accuracy. Stiff\nsystems often favor implicit methods such as Radau or BDF; adaptive\nsolvers assess estimated local errors using absolute and relative\ntolerances. Symplectic schemes are selected for Hamiltonian systems\nbecause of geometric structure preservation; they do not generally\nconserve the original energy exactly at every step.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>⇔<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|1-h\\lambda|&lt;1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad 0&lt;h\\lambda&lt;2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=5\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.3\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none gets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\lambda=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_{n+1}=-0.5y_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nalternating signs with decreasing magnitude. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe factor becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand errors grow. The diagram plots the amplification factor magnitude\nagainst\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand marks the stability limit.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_195_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Explicit Euler amplification magnitude with a stable region below one for hλ between zero and two.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Explicit Euler amplification magnitude\nwith a stable region below one for hλ between zero and two.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>For the scalar test equation, the amplification magnitude\nseparates damped stable steps from unstable ones.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the amplification factor for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\lambda=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is Euler stable with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.25\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=10\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are local error tolerance and stability different?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-0.4=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe discrete mode decays.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\lambda=2.5&gt;2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis unstable.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Small estimated local errors do not on their own guarantee damping\nof numerical disturbances.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The bound of 2 applies to this linear scalar test equation. Nonlinear\ndynamics, multiple eigenvalues, complex spectra and events require\nadditional analysis. A useful approximation also needs to converge under\ntime-step refinement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Time integration motivates the discretization of spatial derivatives\nin M31-L2. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.integrate.solve_ivp.html\">SciPy – solve_ivp<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Documentation: explicit/implicit ODE solvers and error tolerances; Subject relevance to lesson: M31-L1 – Time integration, stiffness and stability · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.336/index.html\">MIT – Numerical Methods for PDE<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course outline: finite differences, volumes/elements and stability; Subject relevance to lesson: M31-L1 – Time integration, stiffness and stability · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M31-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M31-L2 – Partial differential equations and grid methods<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Master: Numerical and Computational Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M31-L1: time integration; M12:\npartial differential equations · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Construct an explicit\nfinite-difference update for the heat equation and interpret its\nstability number.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Temperature, concentration and field amplitudes depend on both\nposition and time. Grid methods replace spatial derivatives by\ndifferences of neighboring values. On a uniform one-dimensional mesh,\nthe centered second difference has second-order spatial accuracy for\nsmooth solutions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The heat equation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_t u=D\\partial_{xx}u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith diffusivity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor spatial spacing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand time step\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe explicit FTCS scheme gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_i^{n+1}=u_i^n+r(u_{i-1}^n-2u_i^n+u_{i+1}^n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=D\\Delta t/(\\Delta x)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe central point has weight\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-2r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the two neighbors have weight\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neach. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le r\\le1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthese weights are nonnegative and preserve nonnegative data under\nsuitable boundary conditions. In the linear one-dimensional case this is\nalso the standard Fourier stability bound. Boundary conditions must be\nspecified separately.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≤<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(1D FTCS heat equation)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=\\frac{D\\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\le\\frac12\\quad\\text{(1D FTCS heat equation)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.1\\,\\mathrm{m^2/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=0.2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe required step size is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t\\le(0.2)^2/(2\\cdot0.1)=0.2\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows the local three-point stencil and a schematic\ntemperature profile that smooths over time. Conservation of total heat\ndepends on the boundary conditions; the example is not a measured\nmaterial response.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_127_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic smoothing of a heat profile and a three-point finite-difference stencil with two neighbors and a central point.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic smoothing of a heat profile and\na three-point finite-difference stencil with two neighbors and a central\npoint.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The explicit heat-equation update couples each new grid value to\nthree values from the previous time level.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin SI units.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to the stable time step when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis halved?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Distinguish Dirichlet and Neumann boundary conditions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe explicit scheme is unstable.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The largest admissible step becomes four times smaller at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Dirichlet fixes field values at a boundary; Neumann fixes their\nnormal derivatives.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The bound applies to the linear one-dimensional equation on a uniform\ngrid. Geometry, variable coefficients, sources and multidimensional\nstencils change the restrictions. Finite volumes make discrete flux\nconservation explicit; finite elements use weak forms with different\nerror analyses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The interaction of discrete time steps and forces leads to molecular\ndynamics in M31-L3. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.336/index.html\">MIT – Numerical Methods for PDE<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course outline: finite differences, volumes/elements and stability; Subject relevance to lesson: M31-L2 – Partial differential equations and grid methods · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M31-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M31-L3 – Molecular dynamics and velocity Verlet<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Master: Numerical and Computational Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M31-L1: integrator stability; M13:\nHamiltonian mechanics · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply a velocity-Verlet update\nusing position, force and acceleration, and assess energy errors\ncorrectly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Molecular dynamics advances many classically modeled particles from\nforces. A harmonic oscillator is a convenient test case: it has a known\nanalytic solution and makes integration error visible. The time step\nneeds to resolve the fastest relevant oscillations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\ddot x=F(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n=F(x_n)/m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nVelocity Verlet first computes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi>h<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{n+1}=x_n+v_nh+\\tfrac12a_nh^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{n+1}=F(x_{n+1})/m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand finally\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{n+1}=v_n+\\tfrac12(a_n+a_{n+1})h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor conservative position-dependent forces the method is time reversible\nand symplectic. It often preserves a nearby modified Hamiltonian better\nthan explicit Euler over long intervals, but the physical energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=\\tfrac12mv^2+V(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not exactly constant at every step.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{n+1}=v_n+\\frac{h}{2}\\bigl(a_n+a_{n+1}\\bigr).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=k=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none gets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_0=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.98<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=0.98<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.98<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_1=-0.98<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.198<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1=-0.198<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEnergy changes from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.499802<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1=0.499802<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure depicts an idealized nearly closed orbit and a schematic\nbounded energy deviation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_314_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Harmonic oscillator phase-space ellipse and small oscillatory energy deviation illustrating symplectic integration.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Harmonic oscillator phase-space ellipse\nand small oscillatory energy deviation illustrating symplectic\nintegration.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Velocity Verlet preserves geometric structure without keeping the\noriginal energy exactly fixed at each step.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_0=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What physical quantity must be recomputed after the position\nupdate?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does symplectic not mean exact energy conservation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.995<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=0.995<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The updated force or acceleration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{n+1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Symplecticity preserves a geometric form; the original Hamiltonian\nmay still oscillate slightly.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Collisions, thermostats, constraints, rough potentials and oversized\nsteps alter the mathematical picture. The drawn orbit is instructional,\nnot a measurement from a molecular simulation. Energy diagnostics should\nseparate drift, oscillation amplitude and step-size convergence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>When deterministic trajectory tracking becomes too costly, stochastic\nsampling offers another estimation strategy. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://fb15.pages.uni-marburg.de/ag-von-domaros/teaching/molecular-dynamics/core_algorithms.html\">Universität Marburg – Core Algorithms<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Velocity-Verlet section: position and velocity updates; Subject relevance to lesson: M31-L3 – Molecular dynamics and velocity Verlet · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/kinetic-theory/\">David Tong – Kinetic Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter outline: kinetic and stochastic models, topic context only; Subject relevance to lesson: M31-L3 – Molecular dynamics and velocity Verlet · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M31-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M31-L4 – Monte Carlo, Markov chains and autocorrelation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Master: Numerical and Computational Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M27: statistical physics; M31-L3:\nsimulation · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Evaluate a Metropolis acceptance\nrule and explain how autocorrelation changes the effective sample\nsize.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Many state spaces are too large to enumerate. Monte Carlo methods\nestimate expectations from samples. In Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC),\nconsecutive draws are dependent; one thousand stored states do not\nautomatically carry as much information as one thousand independent\nobservations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>In the canonical ensemble,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(x)\\propto e^{-\\beta E(x)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor symmetric proposals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(x\\to x\\prime)=q(x\\prime\\to x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nMetropolis acceptance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=\\min(1,e^{-\\beta[E(x\\prime)-E(x)]})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter reaching stationarity, the variance of a sample mean depends on\nautocorrelations. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the normalized\nlag-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorrelation, the idealized integrated autocorrelation time is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_{\\mathrm{int}}=1+2\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\rho_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nleading to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathrm{eff}}\\approx N/\\tau_{\\mathrm{int}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPractical ESS estimators need finite truncation rules and convergence\ndiagnostics and are not identical to this infinite sum.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(stationary idealization)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathrm{eff}}\\approx\\frac{N}{1+2\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\rho_k}\\quad\\text{(stationary idealization)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a schematic chain with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>0.5<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho_k=0.5^k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe sum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\rho_k=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_{\\mathrm{int}}=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA stored sample of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>900<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=900<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndraws provides an idealized\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathrm{eff}}\\approx300<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor an energy increase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E=\\ln2/\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Metropolis acceptance probability is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows decaying model autocorrelation, not measured\nsimulation data.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_182_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Geometrically decaying autocorrelation rho_k equal to 0.5 to the power k versus lag k, with half correlation at lag one.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Geometrically decaying autocorrelation\nrho_k equal to 0.5 to the power k versus lag k, with half correlation at\nlag one.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Correlated draws contain less independent information than their\nraw sample count suggests.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is Metropolis acceptance if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta\\Delta E=\\ln4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathrm{eff}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=1200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_{\\mathrm{int}}=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why should stationarity be assessed before interpreting ESS?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathrm{eff}}\\approx300<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Stationary correlation formulas do not directly apply to a chain\nthat is still drifting.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The stated Metropolis rule assumes symmetric proposals; otherwise the\nHastings correction is needed. ESS summarizes asymptotic information for\na particular estimand, not automatically the entire parameter space. Low\nautocorrelation alone does not prove sampling from the correct\ntarget.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Stochastic uncertainty is equally relevant when reconstructing\nunknown parameters from measurements. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://mc-stan.org/docs/reference-manual/analysis.html\">Stan – Posterior Analysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Effective sample size and autocorrelation; Subject relevance to lesson: M31-L4 – Monte Carlo, Markov chains and autocorrelation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/kinetic-theory/\">David Tong – Kinetic Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter outline: kinetic and stochastic models, topic context only; Subject relevance to lesson: M31-L4 – Monte Carlo, Markov chains and autocorrelation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M31-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M31-L5 – Inverse problems and regularization<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Master: Numerical and Computational Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20: linear algebra and optimization;\nM31-L4: uncertainty · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate a Tikhonov-regularized\nobjective and compute the effect of its regularization parameter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A forward model predicts observations from parameters; the inverse\nproblem seeks parameters compatible with data. Inverse problems can be\nill posed: small measurement errors may then cause large changes in\nreconstructed quantities. Regularization adds an explicit preference\nover candidate solutions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For the linear model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ϵ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=Ax+\\epsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe simplest Tikhonov form minimizes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(x)=\\|Ax-y\\|_2^2+\\lambda\\|x\\|_2^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDifferentiating with respect to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2A^\\top(Ax-y)+2\\lambda x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(A^\\top A+\\lambda I)x_\\lambda=A^\\top y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe normal matrix is positive definite. At small\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe data fit is prioritized; larger\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndamps the reconstructed solution. This penalty implicitly uses a zero\nreference and a quadratic isotropic prior preference.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\lambda=(A^\\top A+\\lambda I)^{-1}A^\\top y\\quad(\\lambda&gt;0).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>In the scalar example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe solution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\lambda=0.5/(0.25+\\lambda)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout regularization,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\lambda=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe absolute data residual rises from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|0.5\\cdot1-1|=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile the solution magnitude falls. The diagram shows this trade-off but\ndoes not identify an optimal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_294_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Reconstructed scalar x lambda decreases from two toward zero as Tikhonov regularization grows.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Reconstructed scalar x lambda decreases\nfrom two toward zero as Tikhonov regularization grows.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Regularization can damp unstable solutions while also altering\nthe fit to observations.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the absolute data residual for the example at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is choosing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na modeling decision?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\lambda=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It sets the balance between matching observations and preferred\nsolution structure.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Standard quadratic regularization favors small-amplitude solutions,\nbut is not universal. Nonlinear forward models, correlated measurement\nerrors, spatial structure or sparsity may require other objectives and\npriors. Numerically QR/SVD formulations are often preferable to\nexplicitly inverting normal equations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Large inverse and forward simulations often require careful\nparallelization. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs4220/2026sp/lec/2026-02-25.html\">Cornell – Numerical Analysis: Tikhonov<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture: regularized least squares; Subject relevance to lesson: M31-L5 – Inverse problems and regularization · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M31-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M31-L6 – Parallel computing, scaling and Amdahl’s law<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Master: Numerical and Computational Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M31-L5: computationally demanding\nmodels; runtime analysis basics · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate maximum ideal speedup\nfrom the parallel program fraction and processor count.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A mathematically reliable numerical method can still be limited by\ncomputation time and memory. Parallel execution distributes work across\ncores or nodes. The main restriction is what fraction of a program can\nexecute concurrently and what remains serial because of\ndependencies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the parallelizable fraction of runtime for a fixed-size problem and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe number of identical processors. In the ideal model the serial\nfraction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not speed up, while the parallel fraction is divided by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNormalized runtime is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_N/T_1=(1-p)+p/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(N)=1/[(1-p)+p/N]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N\\to\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nspeedup is bounded by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{\\max}=1/(1-p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn real systems synchronization, communication, memory bandwidth and\nload imbalance often reduce gains.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(N)=\\frac{1}{(1-p)+p/N},\\qquad S_{\\max}=\\frac1{1-p}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.95<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.95<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.0475<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>10.26<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(20)=1/(0.05+0.0475)\\approx10.26<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEven arbitrarily many processors cannot exceed the ideal limit of 20.\nThe plot compares\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.95<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.95<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napproaching limits of 20 and 5 respectively.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_008_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Amdahl speedup versus processor count for parallel fractions of 95 and 80 percent with asymptotic limits.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Amdahl speedup versus processor count for\nparallel fractions of 95 and 80 percent with asymptotic\nlimits.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Even ideal parallel hardware is bounded by the serial part of a\nprogram.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What asymptotic limit applies at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why may real speedup be smaller than Amdahl predicts?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.1875<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.286<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(4)=1/(0.25+0.1875)\\approx2.286<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{\\max}=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Communication, synchronization, load imbalance and hardware\nbottlenecks add overhead.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Amdahl assumes fixed total work. Growing problem sizes and domain\ndecomposition call for weak-scaling analyses as well. Actual scaling\nshould be verified with controlled benchmarks and consistent accuracy\nrequirements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Computational physics increasingly combines discrete solvers with\nlearned approximations. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://hpc.llnl.gov/documentation/tutorials/introduction-parallel-computing-tutorial\">LLNL – Introduction to Parallel Computing<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Amdahl law and ideal speedup; Subject relevance to lesson: M31-L6 – Parallel computing, scaling and Amdahl’s law · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M31-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M31-L7 – Physics-informed neural networks and PDE residuals<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Master: Numerical and Computational Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M31-L2: PDE discretization; M31-L5:\noptimization; neural-network basics · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate a PINN loss from a PDE\nresidual and boundary data and identify training limitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) use a differentiable\nfunction approximation whose output is constrained by differential\nequations as well as available data. The name denotes a training\nstrategy, not a guarantee of physical correctness. Residuals are\nevaluated at selected collocation points, and the resulting optimization\nmay be poorly conditioned.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For one-dimensional heat transport\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_tu-D\\partial_{xx}u=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nuse a neural approximation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_\\theta(x,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe PDE residual is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\theta=\\partial_tu_\\theta-D\\partial_{xx}u_\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne possible loss is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msubsup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L(\\theta)=w_R\\,\\langle R_\\theta^2\\rangle_\\Omega+w_B\\,\\langle (u_\\theta-g)^2\\rangle_{\\partial\\Omega}+w_I\\,\\langle(u_\\theta-u_0)^2\\rangle_{t=0}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe weights trade off requirements. Automatic differentiation computes\nnetwork derivatives, but a small sampled residual alone proves neither\nuniqueness nor correct boundary physics throughout the domain.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\theta=\\partial_tu_\\theta-D\\partial_{xx}u_\\theta.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>In a simplified sample, let mean squared PDE residual be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.04<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nboundary loss\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.01<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand weights\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_R=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_B=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith no initial-condition term. The loss is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.11<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L=2\\cdot0.04+3\\cdot0.01=0.11<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows the flow from input coordinates through a learned\napproximation to PDE and boundary losses; it is conceptual, not a\ntrained network.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_217_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic PINN data flow with coordinates, neural approximation and separate checks for PDE residual and boundary observations.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic PINN data flow with\ncoordinates, neural approximation and separate checks for PDE residual\nand boundary observations.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The weighting of equation residual and boundary losses influences\nwhich physical constraints dominate training.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_R=w_B=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nresidual loss\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.02<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand boundary loss\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.03<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.03<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which derivatives are needed for the heat-equation residual?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why might low training loss still produce poor predictions?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The first derivative in time and second derivative in space.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Collocation coverage, weights, boundary constraints or optimization\nmay be insufficient.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The cited research addresses gradient and loss-balancing pathologies;\nits abstract does not verify this original numeric example. PINNs are\nnot universally more accurate or efficient than established\nfinite-element, finite-volume or spectral methods. External validation\nand uncertainty analysis remain necessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Every simulation method needs transparent verification and\nindependent validation. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1137/20M1318043\">Wang et al. – Gradient Flow Pathologies in PINNs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Abstract: PDE residuals and optimization pathologies; abstract checked only; Subject relevance to lesson: M31-L7 – Physics-informed neural networks and PDE residuals · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M31-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M31-L8 – Verification, validation and uncertainty budgets<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M31 – Master: Numerical and Computational Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M31-L1–L7: numerical models;\nmeasurement uncertainty basics · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish verification from\nvalidation and estimate grid-error convergence order from three\nresolutions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A simulation may be correctly implemented and still describe the\nphysical world poorly. Verification asks whether equations and\ndiscretizations were implemented correctly and solved accurately enough.\nValidation asks whether the model describes relevant observations within\nits intended domain. Both assessments are needed and rely on different\nevidence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>In a grid convergence study, evaluate one quantity of interest\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat several grid spacings\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf leading error obeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Q_h-Q_*|\\approx Ch^p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith known reference value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhalving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_h/e_{h/2}\\approx2^p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout a known reference, the order can under suitable conditions be\nestimated from three nested grids by Richardson differences:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx\\log_2(|Q_h-Q_{h/2}|/|Q_{h/2}-Q_{h/4}|)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis relation needs the asymptotic convergence regime and the same\nwell-defined output quantity. Measurement uncertainty, model discrepancy\nand parameter variability belong to other uncertainty components.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx\\log_2\\!\\left|\\frac{Q_h-Q_{h/2}}{Q_{h/2}-Q_{h/4}}\\right|.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For an invented convergence example, choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_*=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_h=1+h^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none gets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_h=1.16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.04<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.01<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConsecutive differences are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.12<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.03<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.03<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhose ratio is four, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\log_2(4)=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows quadratically decreasing error across labeled grid\nresolutions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_313_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Quadratic grid-convergence example with errors 0.16, 0.04 and 0.01 for successively halved mesh spacing.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Quadratic grid-convergence example with\nerrors 0.16, 0.04 and 0.01 for successively halved mesh\nspacing.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Quadratically convergent grid sequence with decreasing\nerrors.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Observed second-order convergence supports verification but does not\nestablish physical validation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif consecutive grid differences are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.08<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.08<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.02<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is comparing model outputs with measurements verification or\nvalidation?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are just one coarse and one fine mesh insufficient?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\log_2(4)=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Validation when independent physical observations are used.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two grids cannot reliably establish observed order and may fall\noutside the asymptotic regime.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The figure uses constructed values and does not verify any real\nsimulation. Rounding, solver termination, boundary conditions and model\nassumptions can mask numerical error. Grid convergence cannot substitute\nfor validation against suitable experimental evidence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The module closes with a traceable chain from model to numerical\nsolution, numerical error and comparison with observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.grc.nasa.gov/www/wind/valid/tutorial/verassess.html\">NASA Glenn – Verification Assessment<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Grid convergence and numerical error assessment; Subject relevance to lesson: M31-L8 – Verification, validation and uncertainty budgets · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
